#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d97176-e025-5b4c-b7b0-083e3d586b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 t^{6} e^{t^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561334} {:answer/id #uuid "6c364802-b683-5589-9cbc-78d6e2193cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 3 t^{6} e^{t^{5}} + 2 t (t^{5} e^{t^{5}} + e^{t^{5}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561345} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b596cf-1a25-54ec-ac13-3566c265de83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- t (5 t^{5} + 2) e^{t^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561363} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ce3c3d-48ea-5361-8456-9d24ca042189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t (5 t^{5} + 2) e^{t^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561369} {:answer/id #uuid "e85d9162-b4d4-547e-bd9a-0d23fbc8ff6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 t (t^{5} e^{t^{5}} + e^{t^{5}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3ce3c3d-48ea-5361-8456-9d24ca042189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t (5 t^{5} + 2) e^{t^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561369} :answer-field/id #uuid "95940820-fd45-5f5b-99f8-92f8a12c3bad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf7fe78b-1c1d-5127-987b-8cd96d86abd1" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x e^{x y},$ $x=t^{2}$ and $y=t^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x^{2} e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =t^{5} e^{t^{5}} + e^{t^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =t^{4} e^{t^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} &=2 t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3 t^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =(t^{5} e^{t^{5}} + e^{t^{5}})\\cdot (2 t)+(t^{4} e^{t^{5}})\\cdot (3 t^{2}) \\\\  & =2 t e^{t^{5}} + (2 t^{6} e^{t^{5}}) + (3 t^{6} e^{t^{5}}) \\\\  & =t (5 t^{5} + 2) e^{t^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034ddcd1-8433-5781-9469-3da41704071a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 (t - 3) ((t - 3)^{3} (t - 1) + (t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} + 1) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561311} {:answer/id #uuid "0ce87af5-550b-5dba-a26e-ec8e204c383b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (t - 3)^{4} (2 t - 2) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} + (2 t - 6) ((t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} + e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561314} {:answer/id #uuid "162afb4b-a2e1-57a0-9bd7-26af13560665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 t - 6) ((t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} + e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561316} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8a29c8-9053-5cd1-9ac6-2e79e49f6003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t - 3)^{4} (2 t - 2) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561325} {:answer/id #uuid "73b29452-f6b2-5756-85ed-42f37daa52fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 (t - 3) ((t - 3)^{3} (t - 1) + (t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} + 1) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "034ddcd1-8433-5781-9469-3da41704071a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 (t - 3) ((t - 3)^{3} (t - 1) + (t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} + 1) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561311} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3a4874-dfd3-558e-abb9-8d0f1d1e6fd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1b4aee3-81b2-5ef4-a432-21293ceb4d7a" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x e^{x y},$ $x=(t - 3)^{2}$ and $y=(t - 1)^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x^{2} e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} + e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(t - 3)^{4} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} &=2 t - 6,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2 t - 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =((t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} + e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}})\\cdot (2 t - 6)+((t - 3)^{4} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}})\\cdot (2 t - 2) \\\\  & =(2 t - 6) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} + ((t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} (2 t - 6) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}) + ((t - 3)^{4} (2 t - 2) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =2 (t - 3) ((t - 3)^{3} (t - 1) + (t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} + 1) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28864dae-edc6-5897-84e2-b4f8f19a9ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (2 t - 1)^{6} (8 t - 8) e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}} + 6 (2 t - 1)^{2} ((2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}} + e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561324} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd50b34-1d20-57d9-b5aa-2865814b40a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 (2 t - 1)^{2} ((2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}} + e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561326} {:answer/id #uuid "37d75164-96de-5488-990b-58d49b5c3a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 t - 1)^{6} (8 t - 8) e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561329} {:answer/id #uuid "3f319f38-17f2-56c6-9ef0-8a9cbad2f14d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 2 (2 t - 1)^{2} (12 (t - 1)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3} + 4 (t - 1) (2 t - 1)^{4} + 3) e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561331} {:answer/id #uuid "4a217e14-3bda-5454-bcca-c0092a1651a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 (2 t - 1)^{2} (12 (t - 1)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3} + 4 (t - 1) (2 t - 1)^{4} + 3) e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a217e14-3bda-5454-bcca-c0092a1651a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 (2 t - 1)^{2} (12 (t - 1)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3} + 4 (t - 1) (2 t - 1)^{4} + 3) e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561338} :answer-field/id #uuid "d061cc16-2e62-5867-ac53-ca52bd6a819a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df6ac8b1-d356-59b7-bc1d-0aa4d063c094" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x e^{x y},$ $x=(2 t - 1)^{3}$ and $y=(2 t - 2)^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x^{2} e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}} + e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(2 t - 1)^{6} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} &=6 (2 t - 1)^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=8 t - 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =((2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}} + e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}})\\cdot (6 (2 t - 1)^{2})+((2 t - 1)^{6} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}})\\cdot (8 t - 8) \\\\  & =6 (2 t - 1)^{2} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}} + (6 (2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{5} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}}) + ((2 t - 1)^{6} (8 t - 8) e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}}) \\\\  & =2 (2 t - 1)^{2} (12 (t - 1)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3} + 4 (t - 1) (2 t - 1)^{4} + 3) e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cac902e-523e-57b4-9432-2068e5ed7a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561540} {:answer/id #uuid "94974532-f1d9-5f20-bcd7-0b32d2613dc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561543} {:answer/id #uuid "babf21b5-93b1-5d19-8c90-e1c4da7d5ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561549} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7b438a-f0f6-55ef-b9e4-56bf3f8ff00b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561550} {:answer/id #uuid "fa06a226-9f07-5875-9411-299d674469d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cac902e-523e-57b4-9432-2068e5ed7a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561540} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3bb7ccd-be8d-5a8d-96c5-c31a52f37c25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0cc890ec-db2e-5d10-bd57-1f689423327c" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,9 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 3 e^{3 x}⟩$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y}⟩\n$$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 9 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 12 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{9}{8},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "097b0bc3-3ff7-5783-9b57-cb381a5f6300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561527} {:answer/id #uuid "0b1e8dee-c49e-5763-949a-f2bffd9d1260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561528} {:answer/id #uuid "35e79054-058f-5a00-9694-56f64a66fe01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561537} {:answer/id #uuid "b363201f-18dc-5e51-acfc-f06f7460564c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561547} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5c80ef-250f-585d-9d97-aaa338143d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b363201f-18dc-5e51-acfc-f06f7460564c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561547} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bde310c-c096-553c-942f-54191251cf4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "11c0c73c-d02d-532a-b38b-0bdd208cbe12" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,9 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{2 x},\\,x - 3 e^{2 x}⟩$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 2 e^{2 x + 2 y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + 2 e^{2 x + 2 y}⟩\n$$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{2 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 6 e^{2 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 6 e^{2 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{2 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 6 e^{2 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{2 x}\\right)=1 - 9 e^{2 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{9}{8},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a3c83f0-7ed3-51ab-8487-6a57c6adebb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561535} {:answer/id #uuid "89ade5bf-f111-50c3-b6f3-cc72794398ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561541} {:answer/id #uuid "8f60eb42-ebf3-5e9f-8f06-719b5b2f1b37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561542} {:answer/id #uuid "db053d01-98b1-540c-a231-1f36d1b45f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561553} {:answer/id #uuid "e8311497-7f38-59b8-b2f1-66550399b8ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89ade5bf-f111-50c3-b6f3-cc72794398ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561541} :answer-field/id #uuid "de2b37cb-6e2e-5ca8-bac1-dd5ee5c41bc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54cfa9a4-d8ed-5b5a-b3d9-3810de471254" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{6} + 3 e^{3 x + 2 y},\\,\\frac{7 x}{6} + 2 e^{3 x + 2 y}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨y^{2} - \\frac{y}{6},\\,2 x y + \\frac{5 x}{6}⟩\n$$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨y e^{3 x},\\,x - e^{3 x}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{6}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{7}{6},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{7}{6}\\right)-\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{6}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)+\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)-\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2 y - \\frac{1}{6}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2 y + \\frac{5}{6},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(2 y + \\frac{5}{6}\\right)-\\left(2 y - \\frac{1}{6}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)+\\left(2 y\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{6}\\right)-\\left(2 y\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 3 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 3 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 3 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 4 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c756fc-cb8f-5b03-aa0b-6d03459bcf19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561526} {:answer/id #uuid "1401c740-8e6c-5777-969c-65265545a041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561534} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d53815-0fdc-5f65-995c-069be73f7742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561544} {:answer/id #uuid "b0005b74-6e66-5be6-a3a8-b77b83a97e38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561546} {:answer/id #uuid "b802ac31-d134-508f-8c08-17277103c045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4d53815-0fdc-5f65-995c-069be73f7742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561544} :answer-field/id #uuid "c25cca0a-b7a7-507b-9856-376103dd2619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aaec6d65-7399-5caf-9dee-9a10f698f1a9" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + e^{3 x + y}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨2 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,6 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩\n$$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨2 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 2 e^{3 x}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{1}{8}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{9}{8},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(3 e^{3 x + y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x + y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(6 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(6 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(6 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 6 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 6 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(2 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 6 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(2 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 8 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c00cebd-b9b5-5f2b-8681-11f2690b9226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561530} {:answer/id #uuid "10b86968-4389-5300-b719-86d9e0e50666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561532} {:answer/id #uuid "2a854d42-43c2-553c-8974-9194b1a23ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561536} {:answer/id #uuid "75e2fca5-700d-5fbd-a5db-1006ce585a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561539} {:answer/id #uuid "eab21a85-acc6-5e39-af1a-9a6ca893d02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10b86968-4389-5300-b719-86d9e0e50666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561532} :answer-field/id #uuid "08979814-dd4b-57a0-8acc-a5da4454d4e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e1394ca0-aa0a-5e78-80fd-e34e0b373f26" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{2} - \\frac{y}{4},\\,6 x y + \\frac{3 x}{4}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{4} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y},\\,\\frac{5 x}{4} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y}⟩\n$$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 3 e^{3 x}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 y - \\frac{1}{4}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 y + \\frac{3}{4},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 y + \\frac{3}{4}\\right)-\\left(6 y - \\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)+\\left(6 y\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(6 y\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{4}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{5}{4},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{5}{4}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)+\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 9 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 12 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046b8a59-298b-5353-a076-d3cb1f14b26c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562766} {:answer/id #uuid "2d382635-c61f-5270-95b7-d95a5118de4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562771} {:answer/id #uuid "525e5813-b0e0-5b2f-b02a-34e14302f53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562779} {:answer/id #uuid "53db2eee-8b16-55f9-9f2e-6764503fae01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562780} {:answer/id #uuid "d2264d89-bb92-5918-ae83-5b25039c7085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "525e5813-b0e0-5b2f-b02a-34e14302f53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562779} :answer-field/id #uuid "7478ebc9-6334-518d-87d3-a180009d37c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a3db414-03cf-5eab-8959-e39595655eb0" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-4x^2y)\\,dx+(y+4xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d2/d2ee5fe463ad086cce741c62866c38c7ea331b343bdb0ae82ab64a0f8e730a1e.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-4x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+4xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 4 x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=4 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-4x^2y)\\,dx+(y+4xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(4 y^{2}-(- 4 x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D 4(x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 4(x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta 4(r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(4-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=4\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}4\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=16[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=16\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=32 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e068a41-14e7-5690-b28f-edda3fe55d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562781} {:answer/id #uuid "7991b15a-1528-5b1a-98c0-e9d13c902dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562787} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c88df5-7474-5d10-8f70-02edf6058bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562796} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c5551f-db27-51b2-a4e4-8eeab465ff43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562802} {:answer/id #uuid "ec208b84-8a21-5c5d-ae53-9fe4aaddaacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec208b84-8a21-5c5d-ae53-9fe4aaddaacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562813} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aa8050d-43f2-5b47-aa1b-e9ca68027231" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36d37f77-94a1-5750-955e-00c0cfbaff25" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/ee/eed043488a6cd3949da95907125dcdfa2f946feefff60772495e48e9610c4bc1.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(y^{2}-(- x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{1}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{1}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\frac{1}{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4}[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2960d72a-0a54-5b88-bee3-242a1e3b4640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\pi}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562770} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1bf688-5389-548e-93fa-5d1ff024bc7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35 \\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562777} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a982a0-af6f-52ff-a451-b9eb2139b210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562798} {:answer/id #uuid "be47f539-04eb-5d2b-a660-466609a747e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562803} {:answer/id #uuid "eadab8b3-b89c-5b00-b770-5f624dc9857c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{75 \\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7a982a0-af6f-52ff-a451-b9eb2139b210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562798} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a6ee701-817f-5ced-bd0b-110fb570a0c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65f7dca3-dec5-557b-aa20-0b4ae8f4395f" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5def443330d0f3793a305101ad63ea19d8a6e0a7835a23018063bd6fb57d23a8.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(\\sqrt{5+x}-5x^2y)\\,dx+(e^{-y^2}+5xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3ba969ce97031cecfc6c60ce908a082b268c11a474e6a588b9719ef2ce39e0d2.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{5+x}-5x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=e^{-y^2}+5xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 5 x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=5 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(\\sqrt{5+x}-5x^2y)\\,dx+(e^{-y^2}+5xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(5 y^{2}-(- 5 x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D 5(x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):1\\le r\\le 2,0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 5(x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta 5(r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{1}^{2}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(4-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\frac{15}{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\frac{75}{4}[\\theta]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &=\\frac{75}{4}\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{75 \\pi}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18dc866a-de1a-5704-a1c1-c913f66680cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562767} {:answer/id #uuid "8e790f2e-095d-54af-90a9-fd9fe370115e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562792} {:answer/id #uuid "a81ed047-e215-52ab-be23-78353a509fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562799} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c1bbe4-2e9d-5071-8a5f-7c69fc53bbc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562806} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b4f42c-f65a-56dd-976e-09a6674be37e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5b4f42c-f65a-56dd-976e-09a6674be37e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562810} :answer-field/id #uuid "32e7c13a-cd78-5a43-8f0d-eab158b59557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f78c19f-91c3-570f-9d07-d78ab8ff655f" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f98c6d615262205474643c73e3d4b5bd8d94a8560d0ffbda34ad7aca362d1bd.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(\\sqrt{x+1}-x^2y)\\,dx+(e^y+xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/50/50aabd9d34ec0f889ca832e181e271e3e5918f936708684a125fadcdb1cdb71f.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+1}-x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=e^y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(\\sqrt{x+1}-x^2y)\\,dx+(e^y+xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(y^{2}-(- x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(64-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}64\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=64[\\theta]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &=64\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-0\\right) \\\\ &=32 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b9caf01-baab-5870-b7d6-820bbcfe7d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "162 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562775} {:answer/id #uuid "5f46e0f2-1fa8-54b3-9ddb-9b5bf7e64d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562782} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0d2472-d403-511f-8eb1-f6085c89b973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81 \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562789} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3c6bcc-7b62-526c-870d-099dc45a4c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562791} {:answer/id #uuid "efb5810f-7897-5409-a195-83c50f8b7d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{81 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f46e0f2-1fa8-54b3-9ddb-9b5bf7e64d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562782} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f2685de-a28f-510f-b37e-732196580698" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac95db02-2900-5a0e-a6b1-4da40e53b041" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/6f/6f7b5381fa7792a722e515878be7e2a964d85b9e7f5a17aa83b3983b7f2a7bf5.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(y^{2}-(- x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D (x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{3}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{3}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\frac{81}{4}-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\frac{81}{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\frac{81}{4}[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\frac{81}{4}\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{81 \\pi}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a9a9d6-02ad-5f5b-ba6e-05886838a7e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562774} {:answer/id #uuid "5195a77d-03e2-5c78-823d-910cf0023ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{243 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562778} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d24f9d-8887-5080-b78e-d5e5f67348f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243 \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562797} {:answer/id #uuid "d751fde0-0034-5a42-bdad-6904561a00a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562809} {:answer/id #uuid "eba33b1d-8e12-5cdc-ae2a-8c3766a6a8d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "486 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d751fde0-0034-5a42-bdad-6904561a00a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{243 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562809} :answer-field/id #uuid "419fba28-082c-5d89-8e47-9823b5bee043" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfddd2c6-0361-5659-b47a-55d51d0fa39a" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/6f/6f7b5381fa7792a722e515878be7e2a964d85b9e7f5a17aa83b3983b7f2a7bf5.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-3x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+3xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 3 x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=3 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(3 y^{2}-(- 3 x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D 3(x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3(x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta 3(r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{3}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{3}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\frac{81}{4}-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\frac{81}{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\frac{243}{4}[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\frac{243}{4}\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{243 \\pi}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c046036-74c5-5283-bafb-a0b9acc21ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562785} {:answer/id #uuid "873ce346-c394-5d0c-8dac-2be89d8dab57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562788} {:answer/id #uuid "91d8d73c-4c69-5274-9c4e-28c4d051d857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 384 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562794} {:answer/id #uuid "a97d9f64-2f3b-55cf-85d3-b94f3836a792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1536 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562800} {:answer/id #uuid "bf108011-dd23-5024-bd01-352a6c5ed1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "873ce346-c394-5d0c-8dac-2be89d8dab57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562788} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d781142-e26e-5675-b4b3-e5ca51c4c2e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6252a58-b771-5462-82ca-89e1f399e18c" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 4,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/f0/f070b659b344abe432eb2218cddc02947cecca2e7dafb89a6bf3a49dc4ba06af.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=e^{-x^2}-3x^2y,\\quad Q(x,y)=y+3xy^2,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 3 x^{2},\\quad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=3 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(3 y^{2}-(- 3 x^{2})\\right)\\,dA \\\\ &=\\iint_D 3(x^2+y^2)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3(x^2+y^2)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta 3(r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta))\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_{0}^{4}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(64-0\\right)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=3\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}64\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=192[\\theta]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=192\\left(2 \\pi-0\\right) \\\\ &=384 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e1edb3c-53e2-59fb-8b25-bd49ec07d7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{3} (3 \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563818} {:answer/id #uuid "179e0ce9-20d2-5083-94bb-403ba2113db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563820} {:answer/id #uuid "367d7cc1-3324-54fc-9fb9-97d117004d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{2} (\\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563830} {:answer/id #uuid "802b78f8-6827-5cd8-9458-b108ba1aa055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563842} {:answer/id #uuid "e66f5319-c723-5e2c-94aa-56c9c8ccafda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{3} (\\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e66f5319-c723-5e2c-94aa-56c9c8ccafda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{3} (\\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563856} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba192ad9-824f-5b2a-9948-ce270cf9499b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21cd5573-a0e4-5bef-86ec-1c6e167cab52" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{6}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/47/47ee81cb3603c75c8a83dca2df1153733902576892fa6eed3df4bccd329655ee.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-18{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-18{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-18{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-18\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-9\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-18{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-18{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}-18{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ec3af8-1efb-5bac-9653-b4bbeb227b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563825} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef44ce3-1fed-5c9e-bfcd-9fa6a3cf53c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563836} {:answer/id #uuid "618036f5-1521-5d86-bd10-2233ad57f06b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{2} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563838} {:answer/id #uuid "90d63d22-f3b5-5dc8-bace-ac4440bf2edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (5 \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563843} {:answer/id #uuid "9dfc6627-6984-5941-a167-46191993853d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25ec3af8-1efb-5bac-9653-b4bbeb227b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563825} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dbf35a0-0cac-5fb0-af95-6fe98a827932" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ec57f20-b633-5781-9a09-b50d332a0f91" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{5}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/ee/ee7096a5ce1f420ac962f93b0efb47a00cdc2c9d9ec69f66b6340d02dd106b77.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-6\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-9\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-6{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-6{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-6{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a264f90-1842-5756-9963-c3e6d9be87ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{3} (3 \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563832} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1488b1-b1e1-5fd9-87fb-515370820925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563833} {:answer/id #uuid "9c43a0f5-ae70-5b97-94e4-44c82b646d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{3} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563845} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c4fdfd-aa50-5fc2-9e7b-5dce4c57d69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563853} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e3f26c-fa8e-53df-9b90-6d6c1bed7662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{2} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c43a0f5-ae70-5b97-94e4-44c82b646d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{3} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563845} :answer-field/id #uuid "18e08bdb-12c4-5eb6-9fe4-23f4e1ced610" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5cc10faf-7aa0-5392-84df-19592fc46175" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/fb/fbf107e5d55e2dd51307b821d9d2f5134810317c69706a57f8d9aac528f78e93.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=18{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-18{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-18{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-18\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-9{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b74ee14-79d5-5359-a0d9-6f49e75fed34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{2} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563827} {:answer/id #uuid "36bc7de8-7904-5380-a4ad-90310a70debf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 12 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563831} {:answer/id #uuid "61d95760-e2fb-5368-aeae-0aafe98a48c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563839} {:answer/id #uuid "9f30cc5a-a2b3-5654-bec7-9886e53e4674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563848} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d82cf7-12ef-5422-a34d-58ca680840ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (7 \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f30cc5a-a2b3-5654-bec7-9886e53e4674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563848} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6939241-4a4f-5cfa-bde1-319c8cc79271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63ff3918-bbda-5dba-a275-8bd09f72999d" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/06/06ecd3e3f2b2538556ffb2ffece12564b621f997ef8bf6de0e3297c7b86b393b.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=12{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-12{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-12{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-12\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -12{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -12{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-9{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -12{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1add22ad-9d01-5471-b785-9f130d84b86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (5 \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563822} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0bcb58-3bdc-5cef-adc1-fd56c920b832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (7 \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563823} {:answer/id #uuid "333ff705-2b09-5376-86d1-0978a1ff962e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 3 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563829} {:answer/id #uuid "c96602c9-23ac-55d2-adc5-91df2cf085a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{2} (5 \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563852} {:answer/id #uuid "f00bab6e-d734-5d82-af2a-a6a62be6bf74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1add22ad-9d01-5471-b785-9f130d84b86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (5 \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563822} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8b1678d-9a6c-5b52-8417-f48a18624db5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "66b94c75-2352-5ce1-9d02-093a1a6011c2" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{5}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/ee/ee7096a5ce1f420ac962f93b0efb47a00cdc2c9d9ec69f66b6340d02dd106b77.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=18{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-18{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-18{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-18\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-3{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05641351-2500-5b28-8370-c1b90886418c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r^{2} (\\sin{(\\theta )} - \\cos{(\\theta )})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563817} {:answer/id #uuid "0f9e4b41-f88b-5423-a2a5-30a98f05dfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r (\\sin{(\\theta )} - \\cos{(\\theta )})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563819} {:answer/id #uuid "2629946e-bdd6-534f-937c-a51e26936186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r^{2} \\cos{(2 \\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563826} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9f3cf2-1e76-5612-b0e4-9e6548b89957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r \\cos{(2 \\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563835} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c7afcd-45bb-58b8-a0f7-d3c30eb428c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05641351-2500-5b28-8370-c1b90886418c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r^{2} (\\sin{(\\theta )} - \\cos{(\\theta )})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563817} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f5a9e1a-563c-5f72-836e-afd8b876a1c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "969a550c-c4ff-5d52-a5c3-2f0c19e8dcb1" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨6y(x-1),\\,6x(y-1)⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation? Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and simplify the integrand pointwise before integration." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/fb/fbf107e5d55e2dd51307b821d9d2f5134810317c69706a57f8d9aac528f78e93.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=6y(x-1)$ and $Q(x,y)=6x(y-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6(x-1),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((6(y-1))-(6(x-1)))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6(y-x))\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(6(y-x))\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(6(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}6{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c53d44-953b-562a-b3b2-38f6c6051bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563861} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0b2dd8-7329-5565-a82f-fbdf918e0d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r (- 5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563863} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a670e0-35ba-52fa-b476-0a05dc1679bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 5 r^{3} \\sin{(2 \\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563864} {:answer/id #uuid "ece15ad0-2be9-57bd-af7c-a5fd555eba51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r (5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563865} {:answer/id #uuid "fa596e02-58e8-5f26-9fc1-448d9b654999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b0b2dd8-7329-5565-a82f-fbdf918e0d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r (- 5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563863} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c59553-15cf-5320-87d6-38643dd06fe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02fd691d-ae6a-5f3c-bb90-c16fde492236" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∮_(C)F \\cdot dr = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}drdθ,\n$$\nwhere $F(x, y) = ⟨5xy^{2}, 5x + e^{\\cos y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation? Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and simplify the integrand pointwise before integration." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\oint_C(5 x y^{2})\\,dx+(5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b1a8cf50ccf8ab3a73f31753def3c2fee2143a7372309cccf2ff7a5e68aedd9.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=5 x y^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}}$, we obtain $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=10 x y$, $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=5$.\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\iint_D(5-(10 x y))\\,dA=\\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as $\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}$.\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta(- 10 r^{2} \\sin{(\\theta )} \\cos{(\\theta )} + 5)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{3}r (- 5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31f8d85c-0956-5e4d-bcef-82effb0ac504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565013} {:answer/id #uuid "4588c3b3-aaa7-5ee7-838b-26b6a186aee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565037} {:answer/id #uuid "50711eab-a9fd-54ec-87bd-c694182fb1bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565052} {:answer/id #uuid "9726429c-deeb-55e4-8059-876872148727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565165} {:answer/id #uuid "c7819f4f-0dd1-50d2-8624-6fbce8cb068d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7819f4f-0dd1-50d2-8624-6fbce8cb068d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565240} :answer-field/id #uuid "da5b101c-53ff-558b-b46b-69d15ea46f42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "240c650f-50a8-531d-9d84-394b65ab5cda" :question/math-academy-id "q-128449" :question/problem "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(0,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x+2}{1-3y}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =\\frac{3(0)+2}{1-3(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+2}{1-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(0,1)$ is the $-1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{3x+2}{1-3y} & =-1 \\\\ 3x+2 & =-(1-3y) \\\\ 3x+2 & =3y-1 \\\\ 3y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(0,1)$ is the line $y=x+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d6bf85a-0106-582b-b833-b0e53d86d82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565129} {:answer/id #uuid "c1451722-3327-5387-bdcd-bea771d52fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565229} {:answer/id #uuid "e21eaaf3-ad92-5805-bb11-1e6d8cf5175c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565273} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d78e38-b8a2-5c8a-b4e9-0d71b7c101a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565308} {:answer/id #uuid "f937e620-b64a-58df-bc8a-1ab2197c79b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e21eaaf3-ad92-5805-bb11-1e6d8cf5175c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565273} :answer-field/id #uuid "851cb76b-3dab-5e83-b29a-5e7d934c5feb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f8639b5-fe7e-594e-a639-5523842bd2ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-71206" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{6}{y-{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $H$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3},5) & =\\frac{6}{5-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{6}{y-{x}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ 2 & =y-{x}^{2} \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ is the parabola $y={x}^{2}+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be93fac-24d7-5009-ba04-5e787297aa99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564957} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1e08ab-ab16-5e9e-ae19-5b3fa9994835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}-{y}^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564960} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7e143d-8279-5647-a451-67118e0765ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565104} {:answer/id #uuid "714afbc6-420e-5bbb-9931-29075927e5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565110} {:answer/id #uuid "c1680052-ff70-539b-89f5-342b49b41b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-3={y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0be93fac-24d7-5009-ba04-5e787297aa99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564957} :answer-field/id #uuid "617bb124-5500-5301-bf14-9faf85d05ab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54914efc-dfc4-51a5-a306-07132cb10e69" :question/math-academy-id "q-71219" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $F$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{7},1) & =\\frac{12(1)}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}-{1}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ is the $2$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =2 \\\\ 6y & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ -{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y+9 & =9 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ is the hyperbola $-{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137dcd13-d6dd-5100-aa1a-4ef83376e70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564970} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7ada78-9c1e-53ce-bfd6-73c9704db4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565100} {:answer/id #uuid "8156f8ac-e493-504e-aade-245eb369fa1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565139} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6bc81a-88bf-5b77-b8c7-6ed75d98d631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565174} {:answer/id #uuid "dd82ed50-ec04-508c-a578-c8dd2bbd72c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a7ada78-9c1e-53ce-bfd6-73c9704db4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565100} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d4b2ef-ed57-5651-8502-b9160bd5e70c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "66285a95-784c-5363-bee3-101afc12cafb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161635" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/2f/2f36451290eda3db5221e1a5fdbc1647bbedafa97cd47e22d5523d457c4cb83e.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=r.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le r\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}z({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{3}z({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1210b916-b4ea-5cba-9d0b-88ffd8db5b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{r}^{2}+9}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564968} {:answer/id #uuid "609ff4b7-e3e7-59dc-bd1a-628643aa1eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{r+9}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565086} {:answer/id #uuid "8405bbf3-f8ff-5256-911f-3b037174a5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{r+9}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565143} {:answer/id #uuid "b198dc59-a572-58ee-95d9-e619f5217eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{r}^{2}+9}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565202} {:answer/id #uuid "b3bf8739-96ec-51ef-aae4-6adf4a13a184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{r}^{2}+9}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1210b916-b4ea-5cba-9d0b-88ffd8db5b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{r}^{2}+9}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564968} :answer-field/id #uuid "b54ca112-ae75-5f34-8db6-6d52d5ea414f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6699d568-4112-5bd7-abf4-a27c0cbde6ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-161614" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ for $y\\le 0,$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/35/350b2dc98a09957c17f1ab629e21021c30fcccd9443068c722fe7565f04dcbfe.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9={r}^{2}+9.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le {r}^{2}+9\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1}}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{r}^{2}+9}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a7f360-8352-52bb-b680-11741c575dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564949} {:answer/id #uuid "31f95b5b-7ede-5e9d-8f9b-f6d9bb837662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565014} {:answer/id #uuid "6adb7ddd-b8f8-5210-b2dc-a69eb6349e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565101} {:answer/id #uuid "90fc163a-b462-532a-8c90-4c1d22cba43a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565158} {:answer/id #uuid "9c84aac5-2157-5af5-9026-ef9eb0365bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04a7f360-8352-52bb-b680-11741c575dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564949} :answer-field/id #uuid "30824be3-9d05-5e38-9bc5-879edf4e9627" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cdadd58a-68d8-50fc-902f-9f8e63435f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-129057" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}{{y}^{2}+1}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n\nII. $c=1$ gives a circle\n\nIII. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}{{y}^{2}+1} & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & =c({y}^{2}+1) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(1-c){y}^{2} & =c+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse. So, I is true.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curves are $x=\\pm 1,$ which consists of two lines. So, II is false.\n\n- If $c=2,$ the level curve is $2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=3,$ which is a hyperbola. So, III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fc5a380-1983-5fd3-a9a8-a3c2ecf287fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4(3 - 4 x)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565591} {:answer/id #uuid "73f4128f-a1d8-5bf3-ad9f-85f70a222251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3 - 4 x)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565592} {:answer/id #uuid "95a11de1-bb6b-5eb4-8e63-f0b053e1f1df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(3 - 4 x)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565595} {:answer/id #uuid "c2fd3656-3e39-56a1-92a1-6955b83b3b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24(3 - 4 x)^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565598} {:answer/id #uuid "f881b92f-d7fe-54ff-9a4d-3e96bd197db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24(3 - 4 x)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f881b92f-d7fe-54ff-9a4d-3e96bd197db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24(3 - 4 x)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565603} :answer-field/id #uuid "bad14dfb-e7b1-549d-8b87-2f1d672c7de0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a04edd0-e7bf-56e5-8b7e-72d1c5d80068" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(3 - 4 x)^{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{6},\\,g=3 - 4 x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6g^{5},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=6g^{5}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  &=-24(3 - 4 x)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565600 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37815dd4-eb85-5506-a53b-02d708dc63b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12x(-1+2x)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565590} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca845be-815d-54b6-b943-67a05dfc5659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(-1+2x)^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565594} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a651a1-21d4-5d6e-99e6-82d10932c207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(-1+2x)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565599} {:answer/id #uuid "d0dc0981-4569-5cd4-81b4-a1e05d370b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-1+2x)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565601} {:answer/id #uuid "f30538b4-9ca0-589f-b496-783cf99694de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(-1+2x)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4a651a1-21d4-5d6e-99e6-82d10932c207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(-1+2x)^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565599} :answer-field/id #uuid "a28f42e5-36b6-5f5e-8b58-a45c3ea6040b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c560bc6a-e1e6-5b7e-9d3d-86522a3c4ff0" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(-1+2x)^{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{6},\\,g=-1+2x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6g^{5},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1+2x)^{5} \\\\  &=12(-1+2x)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
